{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "5 - Read benchmarks and plan your first change",
  "description": "Understand baselines, weighting, acceptance accounting, and missing checks. About 15 minutes.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "dflash/cli.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ benchmark\\ =\\ commands\\.add_parser\\(\"benchmark\",\\ help=\"Run\\ a\\ benchmark\"\\)$",
      "title": "Find what the benchmark can configure",
      "description": "Goal: interpret a timing result before treating it as evidence. The CLI exposes model pair, backend, block width, output budget, temperature, reasoning, and dataset settings. Local paths use `max_samples`; the server path uses `num_prompts` and concurrency. Inspect help without weights using `python3 -m dflash.cli benchmark --help`. Running the benchmark itself can download checkpoints and datasets or send server requests. Keep configuration and hardware fixed across comparable runs."
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/benchmark.py",
      "pattern": "^def\\ _select_dataset\\($",
      "title": "Know which prompts are measured",
      "description": "Dataset adapters produce a list of conversations. This selector shuffles indices using a fixed seed of 42. Local calls cap the count at the dataset length; the server path can repeat indices to reach its requested count. Local benchmarks traverse conversation turns, while the server benchmark uses each selected conversation’s first turn. Repeatable index selection does not pin dataset contents, checkpoint revisions, or server configuration."
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/benchmark.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ for\\ bs\\ in\\ \\[1,\\ block_size\\]:$",
      "title": "PyTorch compares two block widths",
      "description": "Before measuring, the runner warms up both widths. It then measures `dflash_generate` with width 1 as its target-only path and with the configured speculative width. Width 1 bypasses draft proposal generation but still uses this generator’s control flow. Results are stored per turn. The speculative response becomes conversation history for later turns. For a meaningful contrast, use a speculative width greater than 1; width 1 makes the two dictionary keys coincide."
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/model.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ time_per_output_token=total_decode_time\\ /\\ num_output_tokens,$",
      "title": "Inspect the clock and denominator",
      "description": "CUDA is synchronized when timestamps are collected. Prefill and time-to-first-token are measured separately; decode timing starts afterward. The reported denominator is `num_output_tokens`, which includes the first token selected during prefill. Thus this exact metric is not simply whole-request latency divided by output length, nor strictly the count of post-prefill tokens. Short generations can emphasize that distinction. Find these timestamps before comparing this number with an external server benchmark."
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/benchmark.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ baseline_tpot\\ =\\ statistics\\.mean\\(r\\[1\\]\\.time_per_output_token\\ for\\ r\\ in\\ responses\\)$",
      "title": "Averages have a specific meaning",
      "description": "The summary averages per-turn time-per-output-token values, then takes the reciprocal to print throughput. Speedup is the ratio of baseline mean TPOT to speculative mean TPOT. It is not total tokens divided by total wall time across the workload.\n\nCheckpoint 5A: If baseline mean TPOT is 0.04 seconds and DFlash mean TPOT is 0.02, what throughputs and speedup are printed? Does the result establish that answers are more accurate?"
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/benchmark.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ mean_accept\\ =\\ statistics\\.mean\\(statistics\\.mean\\(x\\)\\ for\\ x\\ in\\ per_request\\)$",
      "title": "Acceptance summaries weight data differently",
      "description": "The average acceptance length first averages each turn’s recorded rounds, then averages those turn means. The histogram pools rounds. In this implementation the recorded lengths count newly produced tokens, including a correction/bonus, with stop/budget truncation.\n\nCheckpoint 5B: One turn has nine rounds of length 2 and another has one round of length 4. What average is printed, and what is the pooled-round mean? How many draft proposals were accepted in an untruncated greedy round of recorded length 4?"
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/benchmark.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ toks_per_s\\ =\\ total_tokens\\ /\\ max\\(latency,\\ 1e\\-6\\)$",
      "title": "Server throughput measures a different boundary",
      "description": "The server path sums completion tokens from responses and divides by elapsed wall time for concurrent requests, excluding warmup. It includes client/network and serving effects. It prints optional speculative metadata if present, but does not run a target-only server configuration for comparison. Local benchmark output and this aggregate server throughput should not be treated as interchangeable measures. No correctness assertion here compares target-only and speculative token sequences."
    },
    {
      "file": "dflash/model.py",
      "pattern": "^\\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ block_output_ids\\[:,\\ 1:\\]\\ =\\ torch\\.argmax\\(draft_logits,\\ dim=\\-1\\)$",
      "title": "Choose a small change and a falsifiable check",
      "description": "Checkpoint 5C: Before adding a draft refinement pass, name the insertion point, information allowed into it, one cache invariant, and a correctness test. This line finishes original DFlash’s greedy proposals; target verification follows. Your future change must deliberately handle the separate DFlash2 and stochastic paths. Check [the answer key](./docs/TOUR_CHECKPOINTS.md). This pristine baseline has package smoke checks but no added inference-test suite. Do not call benchmark throughput a correctness test or claim a speedup before measuring the complete changed path."
    }
  ]
}
